Proof assessment
-----------------

The theorem requires a finite nonabelian simple G generated by nonidentity
involutions a,b,c, with a!=c and ac=ca. Its proof is sound. Put
I=<a,b> intersect <b,c> and Ji=I intersect Ri for the two rotation groups.
Each Ji is cyclic of index two in I, with b acting by inversion. For |I|>4,
uniqueness of the cyclic subgroup of index two gives J1=J2. Each Ji is
characteristic in cyclic Ri, hence normal in its corresponding dihedral
group. Their common nontrivial subgroup is therefore normal in G.

The delicate |I|=4 case is treated separately. Equal rotation involutions
give a central element. For distinct z1,z2, the proof has b=z1z2 and the
commuting factors <a,z2> and <c,z1> generate G. Simplicity contradicts both
being nontrivial. This handles the mixed reflection case rather than
assuming that the two rotation intersections always coincide.

Thus I=<b>. The endpoint Klein four group meets the two dihedral groups
in <a> and <c>. The remaining subset pairs follow by nesting or by excluding
a generator from the opposite parabolic; otherwise G would be dihedral
or abelian. No normality of I in either dihedral group is assumed.

